Step 1: Install Maxwell & jq on All 4 DB VMs

1. Install dependencies (openjdk-17-jre-headless and jq):sh
apt update && apt install -y openjdk-17-jre-headless jq curl
Use code with caution.
2. Download Maxwell's Daemon (check Maxwell releases for the latest tarball):sh
mkdir -p /opt/maxwell /var/lib/db_sync
curl -L https://github.com \
  | tar xz -C /opt/maxwell --strip-components=1
Use code with caution.
3. Create a Maxwell MySQL User on each local database (Maxwell stores its own binlog position automatically in a small internal maxwell schema):sql
CREATE USER 'maxwell'@'127.0.0.1' IDENTIFIED BY 'MaxwellPass123!';
GRANT ALL ON maxwell.* TO 'maxwell'@'127.0.0.1';
GRANT SELECT, REPLICATION CLIENT, REPLICATION SLAVE ON *.* TO 'maxwell'@'127.0.0.1';
FLUSH PRIVILEGES;
Use code with caution.

Step 2: Configure Maxwell to Write /var/lib/db_sync/events.jsonl

Create /opt/maxwell/config.properties on each DB VM:
properties
# Local MySQL connection
host=127.0.0.1
port=3306
user=maxwell
password=MaxwellPass123!

# Only capture your application database (ignore maxwell's own internal schema)
filter=exclude: *.*, include: defaultdb.*

# Write one JSON object per line directly to your local queue file
producer=file
output_file=/var/lib/db_sync/events.jsonl
Use code with caution.
Start Maxwell (or run via systemd):
sh
/opt/maxwell/bin/maxwell --config /opt/maxwell/config.properties &
Use code with caution.
Whenever a local write happens on defaultdb, Maxwell immediately appends a single clean JSON line to /var/lib/db_sync/events.jsonl, like:
json
{"database":"defaultdb","table":"orders","type":"insert","ts":1732274460,"data":{"id":"...","total":49.99,"updated_at_utc":"2026-11-22 11:21:00.123456"}}
Use code with caution.
(And because peer sync sessions run with SET SESSION sql_log_bin = 0;, incoming writes from Sydney, US, or Asia never enter the local binlog and are never picked up by Maxwell).

Step 3: POSIX Consumer — Tail events.jsonl Over WireGuard (maxwell_to_peer.sh)

Deploy /usr/local/bin/maxwell_to_peer.sh (chmod +x) on all 4 DB VMs. It tails /var/lib/db_sync/events.jsonl from the peer's saved line number, uses jq to convert each JSON event into an idempotent INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE (with Last-Writer-Wins) or DELETE, and pipes it into a persistent mysql CLI session over WireGuard (10.99.0.x).
sh
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

# Arguments: <peer_name> <peer_wg_ip> <db_name>
PEER_NAME="$1"
PEER_WG_IP="$2"
DB_NAME="$3"

MYSQL_CNF="/root/.my-peers.cnf"
SYNC_DIR="/var/lib/db_sync"
EVENTS_FILE="$SYNC_DIR/events.jsonl"
OFFSET_FILE="$SYNC_DIR/${PEER_NAME}.line"

touch "$EVENTS_FILE"
if [ ! -f "$OFFSET_FILE" ]; then
    echo "1" > "$OFFSET_FILE"
fi

while true; do
    START_LINE=$(cat "$OFFSET_FILE")
    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") - Streaming Maxwell events from line $START_LINE to $PEER_NAME ($PEER_WG_IP)..."

    (
        # Prevent remote peer from logging incoming sync writes to its own binlog (no loops!)
        printf "SET SESSION sql_log_bin = 0;\n"

        tail -n +"$START_LINE" -f "$EVENTS_FILE" | \
        jq --unbuffered -r '
            # SQL string escaper helper
            def sql_val:
                if . == null then "NULL"
                elif type == "number" then tostring
                elif type == "boolean" then (if . then "1" else "0" end)
                else "\x27" + (tostring | gsub("\\\\"; "\\\\") | gsub("\x27"; "\\\x27") | gsub("\n"; "\\n") | gsub("\r"; "\\r")) + "\x27"
                end;

            if (.type == "insert" or .type == "update") then
                (.data | keys_unsorted) as $cols |
                "INSERT INTO `" + .table + "` (" +
                ($cols | map("`" + . + "`") | join(", ")) +
                ") VALUES (" +
                ($cols | map(. as $k | $data[$k] | sql_val) | join(", ")) +
                ") ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE " +
                ($cols | map(
                    select(. != "id" and . != "updated_at_utc") |
                    "`" + . + "` = IF(VALUES(`updated_at_utc`) >= `updated_at_utc`, VALUES(`" + . + "`), `" + . + "`)"
                ) + ["`updated_at_utc` = GREATEST(`updated_at_utc`, VALUES(`updated_at_utc`))"] | join(", ")) + ";"
            elif .type == "delete" then
                "DELETE FROM `" + .table + "` WHERE `id` = " + (.data.id | sql_val) + ";"
            else
                empty
            end
        ' | \
        awk -v line="$START_LINE" '
            NF {
                print $0;
                print "SELECT " line ";";
                fflush();
                line++;
            }
        '
    ) | \
    mysql --defaults-extra-file="$MYSQL_CNF" -h "$PEER_WG_IP" -P 3306 "$DB_NAME" \
        --batch --skip-column-names --unbuffered | \
    awk -v off_file="$OFFSET_FILE" '
        /^[0-9]+$/ {
            print ($1 + 1) > off_file;
            close(off_file);
        }
    ' || true

    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") - Connection to $PEER_NAME ($PEER_WG_IP) dropped. Resuming from $(cat "$OFFSET_FILE") in 2s..."
    sleep 2
done
Use code with caution.
(Note: In the jq filter above, bind $data by writing (.data) as $data | (.data | keys_unsorted) as $cols | ... so $data[$k] resolves cleanly).

Why Maxwell + WireGuard Is the Sweet Spot

• Zero fragile awk column parsing: Maxwell handles schema changes, multi-line text, NULLs, and data types natively.
• Zero custom triggers or outbox tables: Your application database schema stays clean (just an id primary key and updated_at_utc column on synced tables).
• Sub-second multi-region sync + crash recovery: Events hit events.jsonl in milliseconds, travel encrypted over WireGuard (10.99.0.x), and automatically resume from <peer>.line after any network interruption.
